{
  "slug": "from-dsl-selection-to-corrected-query",
  "title": "From DSL Selection to a Corrected Query",
  "summary": "A user selection in the DSL is transformed from a UI interaction into a command that the query engine applies to the existing query, returning an updated query to the client.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A DSL query is not edited directly as raw text. A user interaction travels through several layers, from the query context identified by the client to a structured selection command, then through the query engine and back to the UI as an updated query."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph-title",
      "text": "The interaction begins with query context."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The client parses the current DSL query and identifies the token or context surrounding the user's current position. This context allows the interface to determine which values can be offered to the user."
    },
